<resources>
    <!-- Enable auxiliary cameras selector. -->
    <bool name="config_enableAuxCameras">true</bool>

    <!--
         60 fps recording, which the cameras do not advertise the ordinary way:
         android.control.aeAvailableTargetFpsRanges stops at [30, 30] and the
         high speed configurations start at 120, which is slow motion. The rate
         lives behind MediaTek's own streaming mode instead, and its
         com.mediatek.streamingfeature.availableHfpsMaxResolutions is what the
         entries below follow, one camera at a time:

           camera 0 (main, 5.35mm)      1920x1080@60 and 3840x2160@60
           camera 1 (front, 3.0mm)      1920x1080@60
           camera 2 (telephoto, 7.1mm)  1920x1080@60 and 3840x2160@60
           camera 3 (ultra wide, 1.91mm) 1920x1080@60

         The EIS variant of that list is identical, so stabilisation stays
         available at 60. Asking for the mode itself is the app's job, see
         VendorProcessing.setVendorVideoProcessing.

         2160p60 is deliberately NOT offered even though camera 0 and camera 2
         advertise it. Measured on hardware 2026-08-07: at UHD 60 the session
         takes camerahalserver down hard enough that Aperture is killed with it,
         while UHD 30 and FHD 60 both survive. The encoder agrees it is a stretch
         - media_codecs_performance measures the HEVC encoder at 13-29 fps on
         3840x2160, and the only 4K CamcorderProfile is 30 fps.
    -->
    <string-array name="config_additionalVideoConfigurations">
        <item>0</item> <item>hd|fhd</item> <item>60</item>
        <item>1</item> <item>hd|fhd</item> <item>60</item>
        <item>2</item> <item>hd|fhd</item> <item>60</item>
        <item>3</item> <item>hd|fhd</item> <item>60</item>
    </string-array>
</resources>
